{-
   `'` is a token of its own - `doc/spec/lexical.md`'s origin marker.

   It was a *symbol* until now, and a reserved operator is only recognized when it is a symbol run of
   its own length - so `%'a`, `'%a` and `&'a` were each one unknown operator, and each reported
   "expected a type" with nothing said about why. Every spelling below is one that did not lex.

   What the marker means is one thing in all of them: this value's storage belongs to somebody else.
   Whether that storage is then reached by a borrow rather than copied is an *implication* of it, and
   `&` is the separate axis of whether the value is mutable.
-}

data Cell {v: Int}

-- `&'T` is the full spelling - a mutable value somebody else owns - and `&T` is its shorthand. They
-- build one type rather than one wrapping the other, which is what makes these two one function
-- written twice.
fn viaShorthand(c: &Cell) -> Int:
    c.v = c.v + 1
    return c.v

fn viaMarker(c: &'Cell) -> Int:
    c.v = c.v + 1
    return c.v

-- The named form of the same thing. The capability is written outside the origin, which is a
-- spelling choice and now only that: it had to be, while `&'` was one unknown operator.
fn viaLabel(c: &owner'Cell) -> Int:
    c.v = c.v + 1
    return c.v

-- And the bracketed spellings, which are the same type with grouping around it rather than a second
-- constructor. `&('Cell)` built a borrow of a borrow and was accepted in silence by both stages
-- until one rule read both spellings - `Parser::applySigil`.
fn viaBrackets(c: &('Cell)) -> Int:
    c.v = c.v + 1
    return c.v

fn viaBracketedLabel(c: &(owner'Cell)) -> Int:
    c.v = c.v + 1
    return c.v

fn main() -> Int:
    let &a = Cell {v: 1}
    let &b = Cell {v: 10}
    let &c = Cell {v: 100}
    let &d = Cell {v: 1000}
    let &e = Cell {v: 10000}
    return viaShorthand(a) + viaMarker(b) + viaLabel(c) + viaBrackets(d) + viaBracketedLabel(e)
